  emergeParticles: {fileID: 6815250864181084095}
  maskLight: {fileID: 1328518879913614785}
  ui: {fileID: 0}
  wornInfo: 'THE MASK IS EMPTY.


    A masquerade mask is not a portrait and not a disguise. It is a vessel. On a wall it is only paint. It becomes an ancestor while somebody is inside it, dancing - and the wearer is never named.


    Yaa Asantewaa. Mansa Musa. Thomas Sankara. Julius Nyerere. Winnie Madikizela-Mandela. Five people who wore this, and set it down.


    You are looking straight through it now. This wall is not a monument. It is a queue.'
  maskInfo: 'THE MASK


    The left half of this mural is not a portrait. It is a form - a mask built from interlocking planes of ochre, blue and cream.


    In the masquerade traditions this comes out of, a mask is not a disguise and it is not a likeness. It is a vessel. Hanging on a wall it is only wood and paint. It becomes an ancestor at the moment somebody puts it on and dances - and the person inside is never named. That is the point of it.


    So this is not a monument to the five people painted beside it. They are its previous wearers.


    Open it. Meet all five. Then tap it one more time.'
  figureInfo:
  - 'YAA ASANTEWAA


    Queen mother of Ejisu in the Asante kingdom. In 1900 the
    British governor demanded to sit on the Golden Stool, the soul of the Asante
    nation, and the chiefs hesitated. She stood and told them that if the men of
    Asante would not go forward, then the women would. She led the siege of the British
    fort at Kumasi at around sixty years old, in the last major war the Asante fought
    against colonial rule. She was exiled to the Seychelles and died there in 1921.'
  - 'MANSA MUSA


    Ruler of the Mali Empire in the early fourteenth century. His
    pilgrimage to Mecca in 1324 carried so much gold that its distribution is said
    to have unsettled economies along the route for years. He returned to build Timbuktu
    into a centre of scholarship. The mural paints him as medieval European maps
    first drew him: crowned, enthroned, holding gold.'
  - 'THOMAS SANKARA


    President of Burkina Faso from 1983 until his assassination
    in 1987. He renamed the country Burkina Faso, land of upright people, and in
    four years pushed through land redistribution, mass vaccination, and some of
    the continent''s earliest government action against forced marriage and for women
    in public office. He was thirty-seven when he was killed.'
  - 'JULIUS NYERERE


    First president of Tanzania, and the only one of his generation
    of African leaders to hand over power voluntarily and walk away. Tanzanians called
    him Mwalimu, the teacher. He argued that independence meant nothing without economic
    independence, pushed literacy from a small minority to most of the country, and
    gave shelter and backing to liberation movements fighting across southern Africa
    while they had nowhere else to go.'
  - 'WINNIE MADIKIZELA-MANDELA


    South African organiser who held the anti-apartheid
    movement together through decades of banning orders, solitary detention and internal
    exile while Nelson Mandela was imprisoned. Called the Mother of the Nation, revered
    and contested in equal measure, and never quiet.'
--- !u!1 &6045328462661095794
